## How to work

- Choose implementation steps, helpers, delegation and tests proportionate to the task. Keep changes
  inspectable and honor the human checkpoints agreed for this work. A commit or worker session is
  not a workflow milestone.
- Run the project's normal commands through your harness. Choose repetitions, regression coverage
  and baseline comparisons for the behavior being changed. `wf check` is an optional affected-check
  convenience, not a required certificate.
- Address each applicable recorded correction, preserving its source and scope. Use `wf correct
  --address <id> --note "<action/evidence>"` when acted on, or `wf correct --block <id> --owner
  "<recovery owner>" --note "<concrete blocker>"`. Reading this brief or acknowledging a message is
  not completion. Do not edit a person's historical verdict to carry an instruction.
- Record commands, observed results and limitations as short evidence rows in the working document.
  Use `{{wf}}/process/SHOW-ME.md` for compact handovers and visual demonstrations; link long logs. Read previous findings and recorded decisions; repair them through the work and its
  evidence, rather than a prescribed response form.
- Use project setup/reset and diagnostic tools to reach the actual scenario. A check that could not
  run because the scenario or state was absent is this build's work — set it up with the project's
  tools, measure the claim, then restore what you changed, rather than handing the finding back. If a
  capability is unavailable, name what is missing and who owns it; ordinary work does not authorize
  fixing every external dependency.
- Ordinary helpers, tests and local corrections are yours. A genuinely new behavior, consequential
  contract/persistence choice or dropped agreed item needs a recorded decision and renewed agreement.
  Record newly discovered contract paths under `paths:` in the ticket's `## Scope` and run `wf next`.
- For a real decision or missing authority, write `{{folder}}/BLOCKED.md` with a contextual
  `Question:` and stop. Keep changes inside this worktree and respect protected project files.
- Record the review's live demonstration in the working document's `**T2 walk.**` bullet:
  the project's `open:` and any `setup:` lines, or `none — <why>`. A setup line is one of the two
  recipes `wf show` runs, never a shell line, and a `setup:`/`reset:` needs a `ready:` readback:
  `setup: api updateProduct {"path":{"id":"SG-0001"}} as seller` or
  `setup: script ["node","{{folder}}/scenario.mjs","prepare"]`. Keep useful captures under
  `{{folder}}/proof/` with descriptive `before-<label>` / `after-<label>` names.
- For UI changes, before/after PR images are required. Follow
  `{{wf}}/process/SHOW-ME.md#ui-changes-required-pr-images`: capture the baseline before editing
  and supply the final pairs under `## UI evidence` in `{{folder}}/{{file}}` before assessment.

When the agreed work and your verification are ready for independent review, commit and run
`wf step assess`.

Revalidate the T2 walk against the current scenario, including work done after an earlier build.
If live verification made a formerly unavailable page demonstrable, replace stale `none` with the
project's replayable open/setup/readback recipe. A capture alone does not require a browser walk:
a continuing `none` must explain the concrete current limitation or why the work has no live walk.
Use the recorded project-owned scenario preparation and full reset during verification; helper
cleanup alone is not rollback. Report a setup/readback gap as repair before handing off T2.
